        "### Interpretation for Exercise 11\n",
        "\n",
        "1.  **Are the tightest groups in one community or spread across?** &mdash;\n",
        "    The ego networks show that nodes with the highest clustering coefficients tend to belong to distinct communities (clubs), with their neighbors predominantly sharing the same club affiliation. For example, Nodes 7, 12, 14, 15, and 16 all have a clustering coefficient of 1.0 (meaning all their neighbors are connected to each other). When examining their ego networks, we can see that Nodes 7, 12, 14, 15, and 16 primarily belong to one club (orange, club `0` for 12, 15, 16 and red for node 7 for demonstration) and their immediate neighbors are also from the same club. This indicates that these nodes form very cohesive, isolated micro-communities within their respective larger communities, with little direct interaction with the other club through these particular high-clustering nodes.\n",
        "\n",
        "2.  **What does high clustering suggest about these nodes' social roles?** &mdash;\n",
        "    Nodes with high clustering coefficients act as core members within very tight-knit sub-groups or cliques. Their high clustering indicates that their social circle is highly interconnected and self-sufficient. In the context of the Karate Club, these are individuals deeply embedded in one of the two factions, surrounded by mutual friends within that faction. They are not acting as 'bridges' between different groups; rather, they solidify the internal cohesion of their own group.\n",
        "\n",
        "3.  **How does ego network size relate to clustering?** &mdash;\n",
        "    In this specific visualization, we observe that several nodes with a clustering coefficient of 1.0 have relatively small ego networks (meaning they have a low degree). For instance, Node 12, Node 14, Node 15, Node 16 and Node 7 all have a clustering coefficient of 1.0, but their degree (number of direct connections) is low. This highlights that a high clustering coefficient doesn't necessarily mean a large number of connections; it means that *whatever* connections a node has, those connections are highly interconnected. A node with only two neighbors will have a clustering coefficient of 1.0 if those two neighbors are connected to each other, forming a complete triangle with the central node."
