        "### Interpretation for Exercise 12\n",
        "\n",
        "1.  **Which network has clustering = 1 and why?** &mdash; The **Complete Graph** has an average clustering coefficient and transitivity of 1.0. This is because every node is connected to every other node. If a node A is connected to B, and B to C, then A must also be connected to C, forming a triangle. This holds true for all possible triples of nodes.\n",
        "\n",
        "2.  **Which has clustering = 0 and why?** &mdash; The **Star Graph** has an average clustering coefficient of 0.0. In a star graph, all peripheral nodes are connected only to the central node and not to each other. Therefore, no triangles can be formed, as there are no connections between any two neighbors of any node. The central node has a defined clustering coefficient (if it has more than two neighbors), but the average is 0 because the peripheral nodes have a clustering coefficient of 0 (or undefined if degree < 2).\n",
        "\n",
        "3.  **Where does Watts-Strogatz fall and what does this tell us about small-world networks?** &mdash; The **Watts-Strogatz** model (small-world network) falls somewhere between the extremes, exhibiting a moderately high clustering coefficient (around 0.22 in this example, but can be higher depending on parameters). This is a key characteristic of small-world networks: they maintain a relatively high clustering (like regular lattices, where local connections are common) while also having a small average shortest path length (like random networks, due to the presence of a few random 'shortcut' edges). This combination of high clustering and short path lengths is what makes them excellent models for real-world social and biological networks, which often display these properties."
      ],
      "id": "36ce4dfa"
    },
